  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 217 AM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nThursday Night through Saturday... \n\nThursday night marks a slight change in the pattern from the \nprevious couple days as a shortwave trough becomes rooted in the\nupstream flow. Ahead of the wave, moisture advection will \namplify overnight, bringing in PWATs of 1.7-2.0 inches by midday\nFriday. This, along with broad lift along the warm conveyor \nbelt, will support a widespread showers and thunderstorms Friday\nmorning through Saturday morning. The environment remains \nunfavorable for severe storms due to the lack of instability, \nbut this event will provide much-needed rainfall. 24-hour \naccumulations of at least 1 inch are possible, ranging from a\n30% chance in northeast Missouri to a 70% chance north of\nInterstate 80. As is common at this length prior to the event, \nthere remains uncertainty in where the axis of heaviest rainfall\nwill be due to uncertainty in the track. Guidance falls into \ntwo scenarios: one with the track across northern Iowa into \nnorthwest Illinois and the other across central Missouri. With \nthe northern track, the highest accumulations are across the \nnorthern half of the CWA, while with the southern track, the \nhighest accumulations are across the central to southern CWA. \n\nWith widespread rainfall, temperatures on Friday will fall into the \n70s. These below normal temperatures will continue on Saturday, \nthough slightly warmer as clouds and rainfall clear out around \nmidday. The bigger story for Saturday is the potential for \ngusty winds behind the departing system. A tight pressure \ngradient will induce gusty northwesterly winds up to 30 mph \nacross the area, with the strongest gusts in southeast Iowa.  \nSynoptically-driven gusty winds like this are unusual for early\nAugust, as is noted by EFI values of 0.6-0.85 from north to \nsouth. These winds will taper off as the low progresses further \neast through Saturday evening.\n\nSunday through Tuesday... \n\nOnce again, high pressure will settle over the Great Lakes. \nThis will support a series of pleasant and dry early August days\nwith temperatures slowly rising into the mid to upper 80s.",
